<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taejo of Joseon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1335">1335</a>–<a href="/years/1408">1408</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Yi Seong-gye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taejo of Joseon, born Yi Seong-gye, was a Goryeo general who won fame fighting Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and Red Turban invaders. Ordered to attack Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1388, he turned his army back at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wihwa-island">Wihwa Island</a> and took control of the government. In 1392 he deposed the last Goryeo king and founded the Joseon dynasty, moving the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a> (Seoul).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon.jpg" alt="Taejo of Joseon" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jo Jung-muk(?-?), Pak Gijun(?-?), Baek Eunbae(1820-?), Yu Suk(1827-1873) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Taejo_Yi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Taejo, born Yi Sŏnggye on 4 November 1335 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ssangseong-prefecture">Ssangseong Prefecture</a> on the frontier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, was the son of Yi Chach'un, an ethnic Korean official serving the Mongol-led Yuan. By the late fourteenth century Goryeo, 400 years old, was tottering from years of war and de facto occupation by the disintegrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Its court was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, old aristocratic families and generals who fought off the Japanese pirates and the Red Turbans, among them Yi Sŏnggye and his rival Ch'oe Yŏng.</p><p>Yi began his career as an officer in 1360, helped to recapture the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Gaegyeong</a>, from the Red Turbans in 1361 and defeated the Yuan general Naghachu in 1362. He gained prestige in the 1370s and 1380s by repelling the Japanese pirates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1388 a Ming messenger demanded the return of part of Goryeo's northern territory, and Ch'oe Yŏng argued for an invasion of the Liaodong Peninsula. Yi opposed it but was chosen to lead it, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wihwa-island">Wihwa Island</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Amnok River</a> he turned his army back. He marched into the capital, defeated Ch'oe and deposed King U in a coup. He placed U's young son on the throne, had both put to death after a failed attempt to restore U, and made Wang Yo, King Gongyang, the new ruler. In 1392 Yi forced Gongyang to abdicate and took the throne, ending 475 years of Goryeo rule.</p><p>In 1393 he named the dynasty Joseon, and in 1394 he established the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a>, today Seoul. He improved relations with Ming China. No new institutions were created, and no massive purges occurred in his reign. In 1398 his son Yi Pang-wŏn revolted and killed the minister Chŏng To-jŏn and two sons of the late Queen Sindeok, in the First Strife of Princes. Taejo named his second son, Yi Pang-gwa, his successor and abdicated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Taejo retired to the Hamhung Royal Villa and kept his distance from Yi Pang-wŏn, who took the throne in 1400 as King Taejong. A tradition said he executed the emissaries his son sent, but recent studies have found that those men died in revolts that happened in the region. He died on 27 June 1408 at Changdeokgung. Many regard him as a decisive ruler who eliminated a crippled governing system and saved the country from foreign forces, though some scholars, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, view him as a traitor to the old regime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taejo of Joseon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When King U ordered an attack on the Ming in Liaodong in 1388, General <b>Yi Seong-gye</b> turned his army back at Wihwa Island, seized power and, with reformist officials, carried out a sweeping land reform in 1391 that ruined the old aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseon was founded by <b>Yi Seong-gye</b>, a general from the northeastern frontier who had won fame fighting Red Turban rebels and Japanese pirates in the last decades of Goryeo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The general <b>Yi Seong-gye</b> founded the dynasty as King Taejo in 1392, and his descendants of the Jeonju Yi clan reigned until its end.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hamhung, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. Died in Changdeokgung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.97,127.49&amp;year=1382" aria-label="Hamhung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Taejo of Joseon"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5489 4136h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5489 4136h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.97,127.49&amp;year=1382">What was here in 1382? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
